{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And they called before him Avrech.</b> Its form is ha-barekh; the letters alef, heh, vav, yod are interchangeable. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Avrech — a father (av) in wisdom, tender (rakh) in years. And it is written of David (II Samuel 3:39): ״ואנכי היום רך ומשוח מלך״ — and it is two words, av and rakh, like פרחח — peri ḥaḥ; עבטיט — av tit." "And there are those who further interpreted <b>Avrech</b> as two words from the root meaning 'father and master' — from the language used of Herod: 'not rikka, and not the son of rikka' (לא ריכא ולא בר ריכא), because he began as a slave and rose to kingship. Also from the language of Targum — ״יודע ציד״ (a knowing hunter) is rendered נחשירכן, that is, נחש רכן — a master (adon) snake — for most men of the hunt are enchanters (me-naḥshim). Even though the precise Targum reads נחשידכן, from the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory: 'what are we dealing with here? With a man who is shadkha' (בגברא שדכא) — that is, an idle man (adam batel) who has rest from every craft — and the Targum of ״והארץ שקטה ממלחמה״ (Joshua 11:23) is שדכה." "<b>And they set him over all the land of Egypt.</b> Thus they would proclaim before him: this one has been appointed king over all the land of Egypt."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The final two examples (פרחח פרי חח; עבטיט עב טיט) illustrate notarikon-type word splits; rendered literally without expanding the Aramaic glosses.", 1 "The Talmudic Herod passage (b. Bava Batra 3a-4a) uses לא ריכא ולא בר ריכא; resolved the Aramaic idiom literally. The Targum variants נחשירכן/נחשידכן and the root discussion of שדכא/רכא involve phonetic interchange preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:41.176947704Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}